# `integ::AleatoriaOptions`

Sample count, seed and variance-reduction setting of Monte Carlo integration.

`samples` is the number of random points (and of integrand evaluations); the standard error shrinks like `1/√samples`, so four times the samples halves it. `seed` makes a run reproducible: the same seed and options give the same estimate. `stratified` draws one point per equal stratum. `dtype` and `platform` say where the samples live and in what precision, and `infinite_domain_epsilon` applies only to an infinite bound of the one-variable form (the same meaning as in `QuadraOptions`).

## Fields

### `samples`

`std::int64_t`, default `1000000`.

Random points, and integrand evaluations.

### `seed`

`std::uint64_t`, default `0`.

Seed of the random numbers.

### `dtype`

`pluss::ScalarType`, default `Float64`.

Precision of the samples.

### `platform`

`pluss::Platform`, default `CPU`.

Where the samples live.

### `stratified`

`bool`, default `false`.

One sample per equal-width stratum.

### `infinite_domain_epsilon`

`double`, default `1e-10`.

For infinite bounds: distance from the substitution's singular end.

## Guidance

Passed to `aleatoria`, or set as `IntegraOptions::monte_carlo`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The sample count. Four times the samples roughly halves the standard error.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(7));
  for (const std::int64_t n : {10000, 40000, 160000}) {
    integ::AleatoriaOptions options;
    options.samples = n;
    options.seed = 1;
    const integ::AleatoriaResult r = integ::aleatoria([](const Series &t) { return t.square(); }, 0.0, 1.0, options);
    out.writeln("samples", n, ": standard error =", r.standard_error);
  }
}
```

### Example 2

The seed. The same seed repeats a run; another seed gives an independent estimate.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  for (const std::uint64_t seed : {1, 1, 2}) {
    integ::AleatoriaOptions options;
    options.samples = 20000;
    options.seed = seed;
    out.writeln("seed", seed, ": estimate =", integ::aleatoria([](const Series &t) { return t.square(); }, 0.0, 1.0, options).value.item<double>());
  }
}
```

### Example 3

Single precision on the samples.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  integ::AleatoriaOptions options;
  options.samples = 50000;
  options.seed = 4;
  options.dtype = pluss::ScalarType::Float32;
  const integ::AleatoriaResult r = integ::aleatoria([](const Series &t) { return t.square(); }, 0.0, 1.0, options);
  out.writeln("estimate =", r.value.item<float>(), ", is Float32 =", r.value.scalar_type() == pluss::ScalarType::Float32);
}
```

